  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Nature Based Adaptation towards Prosperous and Adept Lives &amp; Livelihoods in Bangladesh (NABAPALLAB)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Bangladesh is one of the frontline victims of climate change. It is exposed to rapid and slow onset climate change impacts like increased intensified floods, cyclones, droughts, riverbank erosion, heat waves, erratic rainfall, and salinity intrusions. The frequent impact of these hazards is affecting food security, water availability, human health, and livelihoods leading to unprecedented displacement, poverty and casualties. Moreover, Bangladesh&apos;s Ecologically Critical Areas (ECA), which are rich in biodiversity, are becoming more vulnerable due to the impacts of climate change and anthropogenic activities. Communities depending on natural resources often remain the most susceptible to climate change.
Sundarbans, the largest mangrove forest in the world, is one of the ECAs which plays a crucial role in protecting lives and livelihoods on the country&apos;s southwest coast. With a population of over 4 million and a rich habitat with rare species, including tigers, the Sundarbans&apos; inhabitants are threatened by climate changes due to rising sea levels and frequent cyclones. Over-extraction of forest resources by locals and industrial development in the nearby areas is currently threatening this World Heritage Site. Another ECA at risk is the Hakaluki Haor in northeast Bangladesh, which is part of a complex wetland system of the Meghna-Surma River basin. Hakaluki Haor is one of the most extensive marsh wetlands in the country, inhabited by over 200,000 people and 558 animal and bird species and is a breeding ground for fisheries.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Farmers_ NBS: 4400, Enterpreneur_ NBS: 55, Poultry/Livestock farmers: 3640, Fisheries Farmer: 2060, Fisheries_ Entrepreneur: 100, P/L vaccination: 21000 (420 events* 50 HHs), Off-farm/Eco-tourism-103, Collection Hub_ 63 (12600=63 points @ 200 HHs)

Early Warning Message Dissemination- 84500 HHs (70 SMS per HH),

Upazila DMC member- 240,

District DMC member- 120</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The main objective of this project is to address current and future adverse impacts of climate change in the ECAs in a timely, effective, and ecosystem-centric manner that also builds the resilience of the livelihoods of surrounding communities.</narrative>
  </description>
